{"duration_api_ms":149730,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"cdd67c86-e36d-41e6-bbfb-a805f2aa1a79","total_cost_usd":0.48714339999999995,"usage":{"input_tokens":26,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20309,"cache_read_input_tokens":354172,"output_tokens":15733,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":6792},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":20309},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":26,"outputTokens":15733,"cacheReadInputTokens":354172,"cacheCreationInputTokens":20309,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.48714339999999995,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":6792,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":14,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"`/work/answer.md` is finished: it has the condition, a proof and five worked examples. sol and argon both reviewed it and signed off, and I've marked it FINAL on the board.\n\n**Answer.** Write d = v_A + v_B + v_C − v_P for the efficiency margin, and assume all values are positive. The Vickrey-nearest point equals the centroid of F exactly when at least one of these holds:\n1. v_P ≤ min(v_A, v_B, v_C). No value cap cuts the face, and all VCG prices are 0.\n2. d ≤ min(v_A, v_B, v_C), which is the same as v_P ≥ v_j + v_k for every pair.\n3. v_A = v_B = v_C.\n\nIf a value can be zero, the two points also coincide when v_P = 0 or some v_i = 0, because F shrinks to a point or a segment.\n\n**Proof outline.**\n- The face is F = {Σp = v_P, 0 ≤ p_i ≤ v_i}.\n- Measure payments from VCG: q = p − p^VCG. In those terms F is a triangle with up to three corners cut off by the caps u_i = min(v_i, d). Those cuts never overlap, because the shifted total m = Σu_i − d is at most u_i + u_j for every pair.\n- The Vickrey-nearest point is a water-filling solution, q_i = min(u_i, t).\n- The centroid has a closed form. Comparing two of its coordinates gives g_i − g_j ∝ f(c_j) − f(c_i), where c_i is the size of corner cut i and f(c) = c²(m − c). f is strictly increasing over the cut sizes that can occur.\n- The centroid lies strictly inside F, so if the two points are equal, no cap binds at the nearest point and it sits at the centre of the triangle. That happens exactly when all three cuts are the same size, and this translates into cases 1–3.\n\n**Examples where they differ:**\n- v_P = 10, v = (8, 4, 2): the nearest point is (6, 2, 2) and the centroid is (58/9, 22/9, 10/9).\n- v_P = 3, v = (2, 3, 3), which was sol's example: the nearest point is (1, 1, 1), with no cap binding, but the centroid is (5/6, 13/12, 13/12). This is the clearest contrast with two winners: a cap that only trims a corner of the face still moves the centroid.\n\n**Checks.** I compared the exact polygon centroid with the nearest point on 20,000 random integer cases, and sol checked every integer case exhaustively, including zero values. There were no mismatches with the condition, and every worked example was confirmed numerically.","ttft_ms":1703,"type":"result","duration_ms":358162,"uuid":"cb293a6d-6afc-4bc5-9eaa-e6d63515703c","ttft_stream_ms":808,"time_to_request_ms":64,"first_content_frame_ms":808,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
